I have serious issues with the translation box not scaling properly (Opera 12.15, default zoom 180%).
The following diff fixes things for me locally - any chance of including tools to change said font size at least? Does the font size work for everyone else as-is?

``` diff
diff --git a/weblate/media/style.css b/weblate/media/style.css
index b0e0f11..a2bffc2 100644
--- a/weblate/media/style.css
+++ b/weblate/media/style.css
@@ -84,6 +84,7 @@ nav li {
     max-width: 50%;
 }
 .translation-editor {
+    font-size: larger;
     width: 100%;
     height: 3em;
 }
```

Hello,

Let's say I have a file like this : 

```
"Source 1" = "";
"Source 2" = "Trans2";
"Source 3" = "Trans3";
```

It will show up as 3 not translated strings and 0 translated strings, leaving the progress bar always empty with iphone subprojects.

Hello,

When trying to save a translation for an iPhone .strings file I got the message : 

`Message not found in backend storage, it is probably corrupted.`

But the file is properly formatted, I just added the subproject, it can read it, but it's just not able to save a new translation.

The file is just this : 

```
//Simple file
"Source1" = "Trans1";
//Other comment
"Source2" = "Trans2";
```

(Saved as utf-16 as this is the proper format)

I would like in component discovery addon to have more control over component's name composition if possible. I have a situation where components names are with dashes and for nice name would be better to have them space separated. However there is no `replace` built-in modifier in django and there is also lack of multiple input arguments support.

Therefore the easiest solution seems to me to implement some `split` modifier and then use it together with `join`. Code sample below.

```python
@register.filter
def splitby(value, sep=" "):
    """
    Splits the input string `value` by given `sep` separator.
    """
    return value.split(sep)

# component name adjust template
{{ component | splitby:"-" | join:" " | capfirst }}
```

The remaining questions are where to put the `splitby` modifier code and is it a good approach overall?

I load Weblate from my virtual. This works great, three suggestions to make it even better:
- Weblate's template folder should be called `templates` so it's automatically picked up by Django (saves me the trouble of having to use the `TEMPLATE_DIRS` variable)
- More blocks in templates to override. For example, blocks inside the navbar's and tools' dropdown elements would make it easier to add links to non-weblate pages without having to override the whole `index.html`/`project.html` files. On the login page it would be nice to have a block in the right column for custom elements. F.E.:

``` html
{% extends "weblate/index.html" %}
{% block tools_menu %}
    <li>My custom first link</li>
    {{ block.super }}
    <li>My custom last link</li>
{% endblock %}
```
- The templates should be inside a subfolder, fe `weblate/templates/weblate/index.html`. This way it's clear when I'm overriding a template where it originally came from.

I just had an interesting experience: I configured [Weblate](http://weblate.org) to manage translations for an Aurelia project, which uses [i18next](http://i18next.com/) to handle translations. This worked wonderfully well, until I started testing with updated translations and found that half the translations had stopped working.

The problem turned out to be due to handling JSON structure: Weblate (via [Translate Toolkit](http://docs.translatehouse.org/projects/translate-toolkit/en/latest/index.html) I guess?) always uses a flat structure for the data. So where my translation.json used to look like this:

``` json
{
    "navbar": {
        "reward": "Rewards",
        "about": "About"
    }
}
```

it got transformed into this:

``` json
{
    "navbar.reward": "Rewards",
    "navbar.about": "About"
}
```

This breaks the translation lookup.
##
